 in Global. Known in the fields of poets & playwrights.", "newValue": "Atika bint Zayd al-Adawiyya, a distinguished female companion of Prophet Muhammad and a celebrated poetess, represents a compelling figure in early Islamic history, known for her eloquence, piety, and independence. Born in Mecca into the noble Adawi clan, she embraced Islam early and migrated to Medina, becoming a prominent member of the nascent Muslim community. Her life, spanning from an unknown birth date to her death in 672 CE (52 AH), intersected with several pivotal moments and leaders of the Classical era, an age characterized by the formation of foundational civilizations and the expansion of the Islamic empire across Global regions.

Atika's personal history is notably marked by her marriages to several prominent figures, including Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr (son of the first Caliph), Umar ibn al-Khattab (the second Caliph), and Zubayr ibn al-Awwam (a close companion of Muhammad). Tragically, all three of these husbands met violent ends, prompting Atika to compose poignant elegies that are still studied today for their literary merit and historical insight. Her reputation as a woman who married martyrs became legendary, with her elegies providing vivid, personal accounts of loss and a unique female perspective on the tumultuous early Islamic period.

